# Bitcoin Illicit Transaction Detection

Final year project — BMAN31260 ITMB, Alliance Manchester Business School

## Overview

This project builds a machine learning pipeline to detect illicit Bitcoin transactions. Given a wallet ID or transaction reference, the system predicts with a confidence score whether the activity is likely illicit (mixing, peeling chains, structuring) or licit.

## Pipeline

Each script has a single job and must be run in order:

| Script | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `01_load.py` | Load and validate the four raw CSV datasets |
| `02_feature.py` | Engineer 17 features per transaction (structural, value, address, wallet) |
| `03_label.py` | Apply heuristic labels (illicit/licit) based on academic literature |
| `04_train.py` | Train and compare Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost |
| `05_predict.py` | Predict whether a given wallet or transaction is illicit |
| `utils.py` | Shared helper functions used across the pipeline |

## Features Engineered

**Structural:** num_inputs, num_outputs, input/output ratio  
**Value:** total, mean, std, max, min output values, value ratio  
**Pattern:** equal-value flag (CoinJoin detection), value entropy, peeling chain flag  
**Address:** fresh output address ratio  
**Wallet:** total transactions, total sent, wallet size  

## Labelling Heuristics

Since the dataset has no ground-truth labels, transactions are labelled using heuristics grounded in academic literature:

- **H1 — CoinJoin/Mixing:** 3+ equal-value outputs with high entropy (Moser et al., 2017)
- **H2 — Peeling chain:** Exactly 2 outputs where one is <1% of the other (Harrigan & Fretter, 2016)
- **H3 — Structuring:** 5+ outputs to predominantly fresh addresses (Meiklejohn et al., 2013)
- **H4 — High-volume wallet:** Top-decile wallet activity with top-decile fan-out (Ron & Shamir, 2013)

## Dataset

Four CSV files required in a `data/` folder (not included in repo due to size):

- `Transactions.csv` — 285,368 transactions across 148 blocks
- `Trans_Detail.csv` — 1,355,890 input/output rows
- `Address_Info.csv` — 984,997 address-level records
- `Wallet_Info.csv` — 331,756 wallet-level records

## Setup

```bash
git clone https://github.com/MokeMaoos/Bitcoin_Tracker.git
cd Bitcoin_Tracker
pip3 install pandas numpy scipy scikit-learn xgboost matplotlib networkx joblib
mkdir data  # then add your CSV files
```

## Usage

```bash
python3 01_load.py       # validate data
python3 02_feature.py    # build feature table (~takes a while)
python3 03_label.py      # apply heuristic labels
python3 04_train.py      # train models, saves best to outputs/model.pkl
python3 05_predict.py --wallet <wallet_id>
python3 05_predict.py --tx <block_no> <trans_no>
```

## Output

- `outputs/transaction_features.csv` — full feature table
- `outputs/transaction_labelled.csv` — features + heuristic labels
- `outputs/model.pkl` — best trained model
- `outputs/plots/` — confusion matrices and feature importance chart

## References

- Harrigan, M. & Fretter, C. (2016). The unreasonable effectiveness of address clustering.
- Meiklejohn, S. et al. (2013). A fistful of bitcoins: Characterising payments among men with no names.
- Moser, M. et al. (2017). An empirical analysis of traceability in the Monero blockchain.
- Ron, D. & Shamir, A. (2013). Quantitative analysis of the full Bitcoin transaction graph.
- Goldfeder, S. et al. (2018). When the cookie meets the blockchain.
